50
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:06,000
I wanted to dive into your thoughts on subscription growth as a system.

51
00:04:06,320 --> 00:04:12,320
So many apps grow as a team and they may have some systems, but I know you think about it at a much higher level.

52
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:19,760
So I wanted you to get an opportunity to really share that broader perspective that you bring to the company inside Life 360.

53
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:24,080
I will say the main point, you kind of already made it.

54
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:29,120
I think the most important thing is for our growth not to be just a team.

55
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:31,120
And to be honest, we started with that.

56
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:39,760
We started with a growth team, but really a system and for everyone in the company to believe that growth is part of their job, right?

57
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:47,280
And naturally, when I talk about growth and talking about revenue growth, which in our case can be subscription, can be ads, can be device sales.

58
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:54,160
We have a Tile and Pets GPS, which are devices to track things and pets, but could also be user growth, right?

59
00:04:54,400 --> 00:04:55,520
Which is naturally super important.

60
00:04:57,440 --> 00:05:03,360
systematic experimentation program at scale was, if you want, the first step.

61
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:10,080
What we are doing now, which is, I think, is the really exciting part, and that first part was naturally very, very positive for us.

62
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:22,240
We are trying to build the tools and AI is going to have a very, very big role there, the agentic tools to allow every team to contribute to the experimentation program, right?

63
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:32,800
And so ideally, everyone in the company thinks the growth is part of what they do, and ideally, everyone in the company allocates part of the bandwidth to experimentation in that sense.

64
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:53,760
And I think that's where it really becomes powerful because if you align the whole company behind top line metrics, probably you know, revenue growth metric and I use a growth metric and you allow all of them to then align behind submetrics that they can really influence and you give them the tools and the mindset and the mandate and the freedom to run their own experimentation.

65
00:05:54,320 --> 00:05:59,760
That's where you get basically a giant experimentation machine that goes, you know, well beyond what a team can do.

66
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:00,080
Yeah.

67
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:13,440
Do you have any specific examples of teams that maybe wouldn't normally be thought of as part of the growth team or like experiments, like is your, you know, customer service team, are they running experiments on kind of user satisfaction?

68
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:15,200
Like any examples?

69
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:16,880
Yeah, they are for sure.

70
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:19,520
I can tell you, for example, our finance team, right?

71
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:40,960
Our finance team automated, you know, if you want a lot of the more kind of routine work and then they build tools that really allow them to mine for information and provide, you know, insight that can drive real, you know, user breakthroughs in terms of pricing in terms of tiering in terms of what we put in our subscription tiers, et cetera, et cetera.

72
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:41,280
Right.

73
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:56,000
So they're definitely very progressive in the sense our HR, you know, people team, right, our HR team, you know, everything from the way they hire to the way they evaluate performance to the way they look at organization has a growth, a growth angle, if you want, right.

74
00:06:56,480 --> 00:06:58,800
So I would say it's definitely a work in progress.

75
00:06:59,280 --> 00:07:01,040
I will not say we're there all the way there.

76
00:07:01,200 --> 00:07:09,200
I will not say we're perfect at all, but the idea is how can we align everyone, you know, beyond our goals and we say publicly that, you know, we want to.

77
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:15,760
be a 150 million MAU company, we want to be a billion dollar revenue company in the next couple of years.

78
00:07:16,320 --> 00:07:21,040
And so how do we allow, align everyone behind those goals and give them the tools to help achieve them?

79
00:07:21,360 --> 00:07:25,440
What does that look like from a staffing perspective, a team by team perspective?

80
00:07:26,240 --> 00:07:32,560
Are there, are there kind of growth leads inside engineering, inside finance that kind of help drive this ship?

81
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:38,480
Or is it just a culture thing where everyone internally is just growth, growth, growth?

82
00:07:38,800 --> 00:07:39,920
How does that actually play out?

83
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,840
Like how would someone replicate?

84
00:07:42,560 --> 00:07:43,840
this culture you're building?

85
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:51,280
I would say that we have functions, mostly the product development ones, where there are people that have a kind of a more explicit growth mandate.

86
00:07:51,520 --> 00:08:02,640
I would say for other teams, we don't have that, but we really relied on what we call builders, which I think is becoming pretty common now in AI native organizations.

87
00:08:03,120 --> 00:08:12,400
It's really people that have the right mindset and they want to make around those tools and they really want to figure out how to do things faster, how to help growth, et cetera, et cetera.

88
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:16,160
And then the idea is to give them the tools and the training.

89
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:19,360
We have programs that specifically train people for this.

90
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:31,040
We have a fellowship program where basically you can raise your hand and say for the next three months, instead of doing my job, I want to go and figure out how to make my team faster, how to use technology to make my team faster.

91
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:35,120
We have different things that we do to really help people with that kind of mindset to do that.

92
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:42,320
But I will say that overall we're looking for them to emerge and self select and kind of start getting things done.

93
00:08:42,880 --> 00:08:43,840
Yeah, that's incredible.

94
00:08:43,920 --> 00:08:47,120
I mean, the term I've heard used a lot lately is agency.

95
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:53,760
As like the best people in a team are those who feel like they can make a difference and then go start making a difference.

96
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:04,320
But it does take the right kind of culture and top down leadership of not micromanaging and kind of celebrating the wins and empowering employees to do those kinds of things.

97
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:07,360
So it's really cool to hear that that's how you're running it.

98
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:19,040
And again, such a big organization doing so much revenue, like to be able to continue operating like that and have that entrepreneurial mindset all the way up and down the stack is really cool.

99
00:09:19,600 --> 00:09:32,320
A few more things on growth., one of the things you shared with me in prepping for this is a kind of equation that you think about the mechanics of velocity plus win rate delivers compounded growth.

100
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:40,560
So we don't think of experimentation as a series of experiments, but we think as a portfolio and a learning portfolio if you want.

101
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:47,920
And very simply, there are three levers that you can pull in terms of effectiveness of the program.

102
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:52,000
Velocity, how many experiments do you ship?

103
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:54,000
We rate what percentage of those?

104
00:09:55,440 --> 00:09:56,000
are successful?

105
00:09:56,320 --> 00:09:59,440
But there is a caveat there that I will go through now.

106
00:09:59,920 --> 00:10:02,000
And the average win, right?

107
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:11,120
Basically what we're trying to do now is increase velocity mostly through AI while maintaining win rate and average win value roughly stable, right?

108
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:15,120
That's the kind of first step for us in going through this AI transformation.

109
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:26,000
When I say that there is a caveat for win rate, it's because the experiment, the only experiments that we are sad about is an experiment that is inconclusive, right?

110
00:10:26,480 --> 00:10:38,880
Then we feel we've wasted our time or we didn't set up the experiment correctly or the hypothesis wasn't right because we took a bunch of time and a bunch of effort and we didn't get static results in any form.

111
00:10:39,520 --> 00:10:41,360
If we win, it's usually a good thing.

112
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:46,800
We call it, we celebrate, we scale it, we most likely also learn something.

113
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:48,240
So we will do more experiments.

114
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:50,960
If we lost, well, then we just learned something.

115
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:53,120
We prove or disprove an hypothesis.

116
00:10:53,440 --> 00:11:01,440
And then the decision could be, okay, this specific line of experimentation doesn't have value today, we should do something else.

117
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:04,240
Or most likely it is, well, you know what?

118
00:11:04,960 --> 00:11:13,360
It didn't work, but it worked well for users that live in the suburbs and have been in the platform for at least a month and have a dog, right?

119
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:15,520
You know, what can we do about that, right?

120
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:18,160
And usually there are ideas that come out of that, right?

121
00:11:18,560 --> 00:11:23,920
And so it's super important then to go very deep in terms of how you look at wins and losses, right?

122
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:33,040
And generally speaking, we built a, I will say, not perfect again, but pretty solid way to A predict the impact of experiments.

123
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:39,520
And then once we run an experiment to really segment and understand why things are happening.

124
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:48,960
And that naturally helps in, you know, then creating more experiments and kind of having this portfolio approach in a portfolio, you know, some will win, some will lose.

125
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:53,040
The important thing is you get you get enough big wins to to drive growth.

126
00:11:53,280 --> 00:11:55,920
And when you lose, you get some good learning.

127
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:05,840
I love that you framed learnings per quarter as a core product metric versus wins per quarter or growth per quarter.

128
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:09,440
It sounds like that was a very intentional framing.

129
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:12,320
And do you actually, I mean, are teams judged on that?

130
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:13,520
Is that like a KPI?

131
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:17,840
Is that a metric that is measured across teams inside Live 360?

132
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:19,840
Or is that just kind of more like an operating principle?

133
00:12:20,160 --> 00:12:21,200
It's operating principle.

134
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:22,560
It's something that we measure it.

135
00:12:22,800 --> 00:12:29,440
I would like to tell you that it's the core KPI, but no, revenue is the core KPI that the teams are judged on.

136
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:30,400
But it's definitely we.

137
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:37,280
So what we do is we measure the amount of inconclusive tests and we think about how we can reduce that.

138
00:12:37,840 --> 00:12:45,840
And we make sure that when there is a loss, we do have the learnings and we do have time to think about it and act on them.

139
00:12:46,240 --> 00:12:52,080
I loved your framing earlier too, that even if an experiment fails, if you learn it can be really powerful.

140
00:12:52,240 --> 00:12:58,000
And the thing you mentioned is like maybe it just works for pet owners in suburban small town or whatever.

141
00:12:58,240 --> 00:13:04,240
One, like what's the system you created to be able to dig that deep into segmentation?

142
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:12,640
And then two, do you then take some of those even tiny segments and do some customized, personalized things for the experiment those segments?

143
00:13:13,120 --> 00:13:14,400
Yes, yes, absolutely.

144
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:24,240
I can talk a bit about these and also about what we're doing with machine learning, which is very, very, very connected to this, being able to kind of look and act on those segments.

145
00:13:24,880 --> 00:13:28,720
That's essentially data science and data questions.

146
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:31,920
We did, I think, a lot of strides in the last couple of years.

147
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:36,160
We have a very, very competent data science and data engineering and data analytics team.

148
00:13:36,480 --> 00:13:42,080
The tools that we use are, you know, I would say tools that most companies use, you know, from Databricks to StatSig.

149
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:44,640
We have some omegrown tools, but really.

150
00:13:44,640 --> 00:13:46,640
But really, infrastructure is pretty standard.

151
00:13:47,200 --> 00:13:53,600
It's really more about, I would say, the understanding and knowledge of the user, right?

152
00:13:54,080 --> 00:14:00,800
So we, naturally, besides having a lot of data, we also spend a lot of time talking to users, I would say a good understanding of the main use cases.

153
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:10,320
And so often when we read an experiment and we get data, it's not just some automated system, although increasingly that's the case that tells us, oh, this is interesting.

154
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:15,520
It's really someone that knows the user and the platform and the product very well that says, oh, this is happening.

155
00:14:15,920 --> 00:14:18,240
You know, I think that's the reason why., right?

156
00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:20,960
Because we've already seen this in other cases, et cetera, et cetera.

157
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:22,720
So that's kind of on the understanding.

158
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:26,400
On the then acting on it, there are two ways.

159
00:14:26,800 --> 00:14:37,680
So one is deliver specific values, specific features that are aimed at subudiences, I will say, or I will say parts of our core audience.

160
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:40,160
So for example, we're doing a lot of work on pets.

161
00:14:40,560 --> 00:14:40,720
Why?

162
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:47,360
Because about eighty percent of our families with kids that use our platform also have a pet.

163
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:58,560
So there is a huge overlap, you know, as you can imagine., and also because we have a lot of audiences on the platform that are not necessarily, you know, this nuclear family couples, for example, a lot of them are pets, right?

164
00:14:58,800 --> 00:15:13,280
And maybe they're not so interested in some of our core features that are like driving safety or things that are, you know, more family oriented, but if you add value, you know, for pet owners, we make the platform better for them, we can make, you know, they subscribe, et cetera, et cetera, right?

165
00:15:13,520 --> 00:15:15,040
So we think about those audiences.

166
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:22,960
The second, which is the most exciting, is that in the last year, we started building machine learning targeting and orchestration system.

167
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:34,160
system that basically does a lot of those things automatically, learns from the data and allows us to target those experiences in a much more accurate way.

168
00:15:34,560 --> 00:15:40,400
So in that example before, will we go and we build and build a big feature for a micro audience?

169
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:41,360
No, probably.

170
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:52,720
But we can know that that audience responds better to certain things and we can target promotions, we can feature education and other things to that audience, right?

171
00:15:53,040 --> 00:16:04,480
And so kind of this machine learning layer orchestrates a lot of the things that people see in the app in terms of paywalls and push notifications in app messaging, et cetera, et cetera.

172
00:16:04,960 --> 00:16:11,200
Eventually, we see that becoming a much deeper personalization layer, even personalizing features in the app.

173
00:16:11,680 --> 00:16:15,200
But we kind of started from the growth revenue layer.

174
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:19,280
I think people sleep on just how powerful things like that can be.

175
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:23,040
You onboard a user, they probably don't read half the stuff.

176
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:26,080
Sometimes you do a 60 page onboarding.

177
00:16:26,240 --> 00:16:27,280
Sometimes you do only a 3.

178
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:29,200
But once you get into the app, it's like.

179
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:31,440
done for a lot of apps.

180
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:41,920
And one of the examples I've been using more and more lately, and podcast listeners will be familiar with this one, but a huge fan of the ladder fitness app.

181
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:56,320
And one of the coolest things they are able to do, and again, I think people sleep on these kind of opportunities, is that they actively have their coaches, because the whole point of ladder is like you have a personal coach on your phone.

182
00:16:56,960 --> 00:17:00,720
They're actually like talking through the workouts and like planning the workouts ahead.

183
00:17:00,800 --> 00:17:03,760
And each workout is customizedize, they like to record a script.

184
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:05,840
It's really an incredible product.

185
00:17:06,240 --> 00:17:10,240
But then the coaches actually coach you on how to use the app.

186
00:17:10,640 --> 00:17:19,120
They like to remind you to like set your weights and then they tell you like it's important to set your weights because then next time we do this exercise you'll know which one to use.

187
00:17:19,840 --> 00:17:22,720
And then they rolled out a big feature nutrition in the app.

188
00:17:23,040 --> 00:17:31,920
And so now the coaches are all saying, hey, don't forget to log your nutrition because no matter how hard you work out, you're not going to see the results you want without getting your diet dialed in.

189
00:17:32,320 --> 00:17:35,680
And it's really cool to see the way that you're doing it.

190
00:17:35,680 --> 00:17:47,200
The way they just kind of pull all that together to get the user to succeed, you know, succeed in their lifting journey, succeed in their health goals, succeed in their diet goals.

191
00:17:47,440 --> 00:17:49,600
And it sounds like that's similar to what you're building.

192
00:17:49,760 --> 00:17:56,160
You don't have the luxury like, and not many apps have the luxury of that kind of a coach in their ear being able to say things like that.

193
00:17:56,880 --> 00:17:59,840
But what are some of the ways that you're surfacing it in the app?

194
00:18:00,160 --> 00:18:04,240
The, the, like feature education, the tips, are you, you know, popping little banners?

195
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:05,200
Is it in a feed?

196
00:18:05,360 --> 00:18:09,440
Like, what are all the ways you're able to, like, push those learnings into the app.

197
00:18:09,760 --> 00:18:14,720
And I think your situation is going to be a lot more relatable to most apps than the latter situation.

198
00:18:14,960 --> 00:18:17,760
So I'm excited to have this opportunity to ask you this question.

199
00:18:18,320 --> 00:18:21,520
I think there is a kind of more mechanical aspect of it.

200
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:27,840
For example, we know like I like every app, the first seven days of a user are the most important.

201
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:42,000
We know there's a very, very high correlation between you joining the app, creating or joining a circle, inviting other people to it or joining a circle, and then being around on day seven and you being around a year later., right?

202
00:18:42,320 --> 00:18:48,240
And so we know that if you're able to make you understand the value of the app in the first week, we have a long term users, right?

203
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:56,640
And so naturally from a product and lifecycle marketing perspective, we have a series of orchestrated steps like most apps, right?

204
00:18:56,880 --> 00:18:59,600
Some of them are onboarding, some of them will be in me boarding.

205
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:07,760
So maybe on day two, we see that you have a user feature, which maybe, you know, maybe you haven't looked at location history, which is a very popular feature.

206
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:12,800
So you say, hey, do you know that you can, you can see that and we use, you know, customize.

207
00:19:12,960 --> 00:19:14,160
So if someone, if John.

208
00:19:14,720 --> 00:19:18,800
is in your circle, we can say something like, What was John apped yesterday?

209
00:19:19,040 --> 00:19:19,200
Right?

210
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:21,440
Things like that.

211
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:22,640
So you can do that, for sure.

212
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:25,360
And that's kinda personalized.

213
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,840
And more and more we're also targeting on specific behavior.

214
00:19:30,240 --> 00:19:37,280
And then the other thing is that a lot of those things after a while become noise because users use a lot of apps.

215
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:38,240
They don't read.

216
00:19:38,880 --> 00:19:40,960
As you said, anybody does, right?

217
00:19:42,320 --> 00:19:46,880
And so they kinda, you know, they tap, tap, tap, and kinda they become noise.

218
00:19:47,360 --> 00:19:52,160
So more and more we want and we need to come up with fun, unique things.

219
00:19:52,480 --> 00:19:55,440
So for example, we launched something called a pet profile.

220
00:19:55,680 --> 00:19:58,400
The pet profile is a setup of a profile for your dog or cat.

221
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:00,080
There are a bunch of reasons for that.

222
00:20:00,560 --> 00:20:06,560
The main one now is that if your dog runs away, you can send a bat signal to your neighborhood basically.

223
00:20:07,120 --> 00:20:10,880
And all the 360 users will know and be on the lookout for the dog.

224
00:20:11,120 --> 00:20:11,360
Right?

225
00:20:11,520 --> 00:20:13,360
We got a really huge success with that.

226
00:20:13,520 --> 00:20:14,560
A lot of people created it.

227
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:17,920
And the way we made people aware of it is we call it peekaboo.

228
00:20:18,240 --> 00:20:20,480
We basically on app open, the app is open.

229
00:20:20,480 --> 00:20:26,080
There was an animated dog popping up, you know, like very cute and asking you to pet him, right?

230
00:20:26,320 --> 00:20:26,800
To pet it.

231
00:20:27,040 --> 00:20:29,680
So you tap on it and then you open it, the feature, right?

232
00:20:29,920 --> 00:20:30,800
So that worked really well.

233
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:32,480
Yeah, no, that's super smart.

234
00:20:32,640 --> 00:20:33,440
And you're exactly right.

235
00:20:33,760 --> 00:20:44,320
Like, you know, the more kind of, the more push notifications you send, the more emails you send, the more in-app banners you drop down, the users do just become blind to it.

236
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:49,440
So it's really cool to hear the success you had with a really creative approach to that.

237
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:50,640
And that's what it's going to take.

238
00:20:50,800 --> 00:20:56,800
And I mean, similarly, it's like, again, Ladder has a unique advantage there, but they're leveraging it, you know, really well.

239
00:20:57,360 --> 00:21:03,200
And so whatever unique advantage or personality or whatever you can bring into it, is going to make it more successful.

240
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:05,200
Any other tips on that front?

241
00:21:05,600 --> 00:21:21,840
I think, you know, contextual prompts, useful prompts, personalized and targeted, if you can make it meaningful, you know, rather than just tap here to check out this feature, something about, you know, their profile, something that is relevant to them, that's really the way to go.

242
00:21:21,920 --> 00:21:26,160
You're really needing to cut through the noise and make it very clear why it's interesting, why it's cool, what it is.

243
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:27,120
Why is it cool, why is it fun.

244
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:27,680
That's awesome.

245
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:42,240
And the cool thing too is like with these AI tools, it's hoped it's going to get easier and easier for even smaller apps and smaller teams to do this kind of personalization at scale, where it's, you know, probably taking you all years and machine learning team and data science team to get there.

246
00:21:42,400 --> 00:21:48,960
I think it's going to get easier and easier for smaller and smaller teams and smaller and smaller apps to do this level of personalization, which is cool.

247
00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:49,280
Yeah.

248
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:50,320
Exciting.

249
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:54,000
So the next thing I wanted to talk about is your take on freemium.

250
00:21:54,240 --> 00:21:55,840
And it's been such a hot topic lately.

251
00:21:56,560 --> 00:21:59,040
We've had guests on the podcast talking about it recently.

252
00:21:59,600 --> 00:22:11,680
And then, you know, in the state of subscription apps report, we actually shared a number that blown a lot of people away, myself included, that hard paywall apps convert users at five times the rate of freemium apps.

253
00:22:11,840 --> 00:22:18,480
And I think a lot of people in the industry took that as a, you know, hard paywall or die, like that's the only way to grow as a subscription app.

254
00:22:18,720 --> 00:22:25,040
And maybe that's true for, like, smaller apps, but that's obviously not true of an app at Life 360 scale.

255
00:22:25,200 --> 00:22:26,720
So what do you think about freemium?

256
00:22:27,040 --> 00:22:30,000
So first of all, it depends on the type of app, right?

257
00:22:30,080 --> 00:22:33,760
There are, you know, some apps for which hard pay will absolutely make sense.

258
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:39,200
You know, apps where users have very high conviction, they're not social, they don't benefit from network effect, et cetera, et cetera.

259
00:22:39,520 --> 00:22:41,040
So that makes a lot of sense, right?

260
00:22:41,280 --> 00:22:48,320
For us, you know, we not only are a freemium app, but our free tier is extremely generous.

261
00:22:48,720 --> 00:22:52,960
I will say that for most people, it's good, it's great, it's good enough, right?

262
00:22:53,200 --> 00:22:59,120
In fact, the reason number one why people tell us they don't subscribe is because the free tier is good enough.

263
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:11,600
And our philosophy is that we want to do something about it, but that something is not making the free tier worse, is trying to provide more value and really diversify the subscription offering, right?

264
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:12,400
Why?

265
00:23:12,960 --> 00:23:13,760
There are a few reasons.

266
00:23:14,080 --> 00:23:27,920
One is, well, our company mission, we're really all about keeping families safe and we have a lot of features that are SOS and crash detection, et cetera, et cetera, that are literally life saving features in some cases.

267
00:23:28,320 --> 00:23:33,600
And so making it accessible to everyone is a big part of why we exist, really.

268
00:23:34,320 --> 00:23:43,120
But the second is we are an app that benefits from network effect in life 36 in solo mode is not really useful or interesting, right?

269
00:23:43,520 --> 00:23:46,480
You need to invite your family, invite your friends, et cetera, et cetera.

270
00:23:46,960 --> 00:23:52,720
The number one way that people discover us is parents telling other parents about the app.

271
00:23:53,920 --> 00:24:07,200
And so, you know, our thesis is that having a large free engage a free user base around the world is actually one of the big modes and one of the big competitive advantages for us.

272
00:24:07,440 --> 00:24:09,600
I will also say that, you know, we have an advertising business.

273
00:24:09,920 --> 00:24:10,880
So naturally, there is also.

274
00:24:11,600 --> 00:24:13,920
a business incentive for us to grow our free base.

275
00:24:14,560 --> 00:24:30,240
But it's really, you know, the more people in Light 360, the more people invite more people, the more people know about it, the more features like the PetFinder network or the Tile devices or other features, the safety features that we have in the app are useful, you know, the more density of users.

276
00:24:30,640 --> 00:24:32,560
And so, you know, for us, it's all about that.

277
00:24:33,040 --> 00:24:42,240
That said, you know, as I was saying before, about three percent of our MAU are subscribers, but on average, about four people use a subscription.

278
00:24:42,560 --> 00:24:46,960
So it's really more about twelve percent of our MAU that a benefit for subscription, right?

279
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:51,200
Which is, I would say, in line with with a lot of subscription apps.

280
00:24:51,760 --> 00:25:07,680
We are able to provide a good free experience, a great free experience, and certainly we could limit that and grow a subscriber base much faster, but I think that we will pay for that down the line in lower growth and becoming a less interesting product.

281
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:08,080
Yeah.

282
00:25:08,240 --> 00:25:08,960
How do you think about that?

283
00:25:09,120 --> 00:25:19,440
Like, how do you think about when to add friction to the free experience versus when to, like you said?, just think only about adding value.

284
00:25:19,920 --> 00:25:20,400
Well, yeah.

285
00:25:20,480 --> 00:25:23,280
So adding friction to the free experience, we try not to.

286
00:25:23,840 --> 00:25:26,240
Naturally, in some cases, you have to.

287
00:25:26,640 --> 00:25:30,160
For example, we have a paywall in onboarding, right?

288
00:25:30,720 --> 00:25:31,440
Like every other app.

289
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:40,560
Sure, it does add, you know, one screen of friction, but not having that will cut off a significant amount of other new subscribers, right?

290
00:25:40,880 --> 00:25:53,360
We have a feature that is location history where you can look at for the last year two days for free users where people in your circle were.

291
00:25:53,760 --> 00:25:56,320
Two days is more than enough for a lot of use cases.

292
00:25:56,640 --> 00:25:59,280
Usually people want to see where people were in the last day or so.

293
00:25:59,920 --> 00:26:01,120
We did a test in the past.

294
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:07,600
If you cut it down to six hours or even twelve hours, we double the number of subscribers from the hook.

295
00:26:08,320 --> 00:26:10,880
So it will be a sizable win.

296
00:26:11,600 --> 00:26:20,800
A lot of companies will take that, but we haven't, because it does make the feature significantly less appealing for free users.

297
00:26:21,120 --> 00:26:22,800
And it does make it less useful.

298
00:26:23,920 --> 00:26:26,560
and in some cases it can make people less safe.

299
00:26:26,720 --> 00:26:28,080
So we decided not to do it.

300
00:26:28,480 --> 00:26:35,680
And, you know, we paid for it on short term bottom line, but we think in the long term it's going to be, you know, the right thing to do for a business.

301
00:26:36,160 --> 00:26:41,840
It's so hard to balance the short term wins against a long term strategy.

302
00:26:42,960 --> 00:26:48,240
Yeah, I mean, I talked to the Chief Product Officer of Duolingo about this when I chatted with him earlier this year.

303
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:55,600
And they think along those same lines is that, you know, I think he called it the premium trap is that you can easily.

304
00:26:56,080 --> 00:27:05,520
juice numbers and hit your quarterly revenue by just locking more and more features behind the premium tier, but then you're really shooting yourself in the foot long term.

305
00:27:05,920 --> 00:27:08,080
But it's so hard to get that balance right.

306
00:27:08,400 --> 00:27:19,440
Any advice you have on how you think about how you differentiate, even things like when you create a new feature, what's the debate internally, whether it's a premium feature or a free feature?

307
00:27:19,840 --> 00:27:21,200
How do you make those kinds of decisions?

308
00:27:21,360 --> 00:27:22,160
Because it's so hard.

309
00:27:22,560 --> 00:27:24,400
Yeah, it's a very interesting one.

310
00:27:24,640 --> 00:27:28,560
And it's actually something that we've been wrestling with because historically we had.

311
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:38,640
teams thinking about a free experience and teams, which are the ones I work with now a lot, thinking more about the paid experience.

312
00:27:39,280 --> 00:27:51,040
And that's actually not the right way to look at this because in most cases we think that unless there's a reason and the reason is usually cost.

313
00:27:51,520 --> 00:27:54,720
For example, we offer roadside assistance to our subscribers, right?

314
00:27:54,960 --> 00:28:00,560
We could not offer roadside assistance to our free users because every time they call it, we pay for the toll, right?

315
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:08,000
And so that's one where, yes, it's a paid feature and I think nobody expects to get, you know, a Rosetta system for free, right?

316
00:28:08,640 --> 00:28:21,760
In some other cases, we think that every feature should be freemium, meaning that every feature that provides value to a user should be great in the free version of the feature itself.

317
00:28:22,320 --> 00:28:29,120
And then it's up to us find value, find additional value, real value that we can provide as a premium, right?

318
00:28:29,680 --> 00:28:34,640
The challenge in most cases is thinking of the premium part of a premium.

319
00:28:34,640 --> 00:29:09,120
a freemium not as an artificial gate you know say i give you two for free and then the rest is paid although some features that we have worked away but the challenge that we are wrestling most times is how do we make something free and then create a completely new experience a completely new tier on top of that that is that is what we're paying for right and so part of this is getting teams that always think about free to think about both and vice versa right and it's an interesting debate that we have.

320
00:29:09,760 --> 00:29:17,600
There's no single question, but I will say, generally speaking, if something is very expensive to provide, it will become a premium feature.

321
00:29:18,560 --> 00:29:28,240
If something is more of a software based feature, we start with three, we try to build the best we can, and then we find a creative way to build on top of it.

322
00:29:29,120 --> 00:29:42,480
How do you think about or measure how that factors into long term growth, especially compared to, you know, customer acquisition and growth is like it's easier, it's easier, it's not easy these days at Post ATT, but it's easier to measure.

323
00:29:42,560 --> 00:29:43,840
It's easier to see the numbers.

324
00:29:44,480 --> 00:29:48,400
How are you tracking that like subscriber value that you create?

325
00:29:48,640 --> 00:29:55,200
How are you thinking about growing that and its impact on revenue when it's so much easier to track other things?

326
00:29:55,520 --> 00:29:59,520
I mean, there are literally different ways in which we track value.

327
00:29:59,760 --> 00:30:01,520
The natural one is LTV, right?

328
00:30:02,400 --> 00:30:07,280
And so like every company, we have an LTV model that is all evolving.

329
00:30:07,600 --> 00:30:18,000
In our case, it's quite complex because you need to take a subscription into consideration, you need to take device sales into consideration, you need to take advertising into consideration.

330
00:30:18,400 --> 00:30:22,640
If you want to get fancy, you need to take the viral factor into consideration.

331
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:23,360
Right.

332
00:30:25,040 --> 00:30:27,840
And so it's a beast that is ever evolving.

333
00:30:29,760 --> 00:30:39,360
But Natalie, you know, that's kind of, you know, the big one that works, you know, in the background and we use it, you know, then we attribute different campaigns to it, et cetera, et cetera.

334
00:30:39,600 --> 00:30:49,360
The reality is that if you want to narrow it out to subscription, it's really a factor of how many users we convert to subscription, what package do they choose?

335
00:30:49,840 --> 00:30:56,080
Silver, gold, platinum, they're nettily different value and different economics, and how long they stay subscribed, right?

336
00:30:56,480 --> 00:31:07,440
And so, in reality, the job is pretty straightforward in the sense is to get as many people as possible to subscribe, direct them to the best package for them, and then try to keep them subscribed as long as possible.

337
00:31:08,080 --> 00:31:08,800
It is such a shame.

338
00:31:09,040 --> 00:31:28,000
I do feel like there are a lot of apps that get to this kind of place product wise where it's like they hit product market fit, and then they just put all the gas into growth and top of a funnel and are not putting as much effort, attention, focus into continuing to like increase that value over time.

339
00:31:28,160 --> 00:31:41,840
And so it's really cool to hear that as a kind of primary focus of the company, not just, you know, that top line growth where it's easier to see, but it doesn't build that momentum that really compounds over time.

340
00:31:42,240 --> 00:31:52,400
Earlier you mentioned how Life 360 really is better with a family, with as a couple, with a friend group or something.

341
00:31:53,680 --> 00:32:01,440
How do you think about Li 360 as that kind of almost a social album versus like a standard?

342
00:32:01,840 --> 00:32:04,640
I know you've worked on a lot of subscription apps in your career.

343
00:32:05,680 --> 00:32:07,840
So how does it differ and how do you think about that?

344
00:32:08,160 --> 00:32:12,240
It's interesting, I think, because we're not a social app, right?

345
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:13,520
We're not a social network.

346
00:32:14,000 --> 00:32:19,840
We're not a place where you can actually communicate with people outside of your core circle.

347
00:32:20,160 --> 00:32:22,960
Or you can communicate explicitly in a match.

348
00:32:23,440 --> 00:32:24,640
You can send emojis and things like that.

349
00:32:24,720 --> 00:32:28,400
You can check on people., you know, but there's no explicit communication.

350
00:32:28,720 --> 00:32:32,480
There's no big viral kind of mechanic, right?

351
00:32:33,280 --> 00:32:40,960
It's not an app where, I don't know, you're going to share your location on X, you know, for everyone to see where you are, right?

352
00:32:41,280 --> 00:32:52,320
And so it's a it's a social app in the sense that the circle, which is often a family, but can be anything really, is the unit, right?

353
00:32:53,200 --> 00:32:54,240
The circle is the core unit.

354
00:32:54,400 --> 00:32:58,320
And as I was saying, a paying circle on average is about four people a circle.

355
00:32:58,320 --> 00:33:00,000
Circle on average of about three people.

356
00:33:00,400 --> 00:33:03,200
It changes everything we do.

357
00:33:03,680 --> 00:33:05,120
It doesn't just change monetization.

358
00:33:05,360 --> 00:33:07,840
It doesn't change the way we think about growth.

359
00:33:08,160 --> 00:33:15,520
For example, if we launch, if we think of a new feature, often the conversation will be, you know, we have an idea for this feature.

360
00:33:15,840 --> 00:33:18,400
And say, okay, well, that's a good idea.

361
00:33:18,800 --> 00:33:24,000
But I don't know, Snapchat is something similar or Instagram is something similar.

362
00:33:24,320 --> 00:33:25,440
You know, how is that different?

363
00:33:26,240 --> 00:33:33,680
You know, and often the answer is, well, because we're providing valueue to the circle in this way, in that way, in that way.

364
00:33:34,480 --> 00:33:41,760
And so it makes things harder in a way because you're a bit more constrained in what you can do to grow.

365
00:33:42,400 --> 00:33:50,160
But it also provides clarity in other ways because it makes easier to understand this is something that we should be doing or not doing.

366
00:33:50,480 --> 00:33:53,200
This is how we build something that is really unique to us.

367
00:33:54,000 --> 00:33:57,200
How do you think about and measure those halo effects?

368
00:33:57,280 --> 00:34:01,520
Because you talked about, like, on average, only one person is subscribing for four people using.

369
00:34:02,480 --> 00:34:13,840
Are you watching those individual users who are not the subscriber, that when it goes from four to two, is that like a signal that the one subscriber is potentially going to churn?

370
00:34:14,640 --> 00:34:21,120
I imagine it adds a lot of complexity in the kind of understanding usage patterns and retention and subscriber retention.

371
00:34:22,160 --> 00:34:45,600
Without going into a lot of detail, but what I can say is that there is a certain circle size, three to four people, that has significantly higher conversion and retention than lower and higher circle sizeszes are often friend groups or things like extended family or they're probably less motivated to pay for safety features.

372
00:34:45,920 --> 00:34:56,720
And lower secure sizes, yes, they're either couples or maybe groups that, you know, maybe the kids, you know, one thing that one big churn reason that we have is, you know, the kids left, they're in college.

373
00:34:58,480 --> 00:35:04,160
And, you know, and that's basically for some families at least the end of the LCC circle, right?

374
00:35:04,640 --> 00:35:08,400
It's really about, you know, how their needs and how their life changes.

375
00:35:08,800 --> 00:35:08,880
Yeah.

376
00:35:09,120 --> 00:35:11,040
Well, that's great., the pet feature, right?

377
00:35:11,280 --> 00:35:17,280
So the kids leave house and now you're extending that runway by tracking their pet instead of the kids.

378
00:35:17,520 --> 00:35:18,480
Yeah, it's not going to be good.

379
00:35:20,720 --> 00:35:39,760
I did want to dig into that actually because it is fairly unique as a company in that you have physical products that you sell, the tile brand that was that Life 360 purchased, which really cool now is the Apple find my compatible and all those things.

380
00:35:39,920 --> 00:35:41,360
So that's a really fantastic product.

381
00:35:41,600 --> 00:35:43,040
And then now the pet product that was.

382
00:35:43,520 --> 00:35:44,560
released not too long ago.

